# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECRETS — every .env is ignored by DEFAULT, with no exceptions.
#
# This is a lesson, not boilerplate: in the previous repo `.env.dev` and
# `apps/app/.env.migration-release` were both TRACKED. A negated allow-list
# (`!.env.example`) is how that happens — one wrong pattern and a real secret
# is committed forever. So: no negations here. If you need an example file,
# name it `env.example` (no leading dot) so it cannot match these patterns.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
*.env
.envrc
.npmrc
.infisical.json

# Keys and certificates
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.p8
*.mobileprovision
*.der
*.asc
id_rsa
id_dsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Agent tooling. Anthropic Agent Skills must never be committed — their licence
# forbids retaining copies outside Anthropic's Services. Scoped to the agent
# install directories rather than a bare `skills/`, which would also match
# product assets.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.claude/skills/*
.claude/worktrees/
# Skills AUTHORED IN THIS REPO are not Anthropic's and are un-ignored one by one,
# so the blanket above keeps catching anything installed rather than written here.
!.claude/skills/ship/
!.claude/skills/ship/**
.agent/skills/
.agents/skills/
.cursor/skills/
.windsurf/skills/
.opencode/skills/

# Derived artefacts — rebuildable from the tree, so never committed.
graphify-out/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Personal / user data. Never in the repo.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A self-hoster's own leaf document — a real person's portfolio and the evidence behind it.
# `leaf.json` is the default path apps/leaf reads, so it WILL exist on a working install;
# ignoring it by NAME, at any depth, is what keeps it from being committed by accident. The
# bundled illustration lives at apps/leaf/example/leaf.example.json instead, declares itself an
# example, and is loaded only when FOLIAGE_LEAF names it.

# ONE ANCHORED PATH PER PRODUCER WAS NOT ENOUGH, which is why the names below are unanchored.
# Both producers in this repo write real personal data to caller-chosen locations:
#   - `intake.mjs --out <path>` writes a leaf document ANYWHERE the operator names;
#   - `FOLIAGE_TREE=<path>` points the canopy at a tree manifest ANYWHERE, and that manifest
#     names further leaf documents resolved relative to itself.
# So the default path was covered and every other real artifact was not. A leaf document holds
# a real person's name, their email identity and up to hundreds of verbatim commit titles; a
# tree manifest holds an organization's roster. Neither may ever be committed.
#
# No ignore file can cover a path chosen at run time, so the defence is that every NAME these
# tools write is ignored EVERYWHERE — both the dotted convention the docs teach and the bare
# default the tools actually write — so the documented way of writing one is safe by default
# wherever it is written.
#
# IT USED TO BE TWO LAYERS AND THE SECOND ONE HAD A HOLE. The default locations were anchored
# (`/apps/leaf/leaf.json`, `/tree.json`, and four more) while only the DOTTED forms were ignored
# everywhere, so the same document one directory over from its default was committable — measured
# 2026-09-04: `apps/canopy/deploy/tree.json` NOT IGNORED, found while preparing a deployment whose
# plan puts a real roster at exactly that path. Unanchoring the bare names closes the hole and
# makes those six anchors dead letters, so they are DELETED rather than left to read as though
# they were still carrying something. `gitignoreDefence.test.mjs` asserts the outcome directly. Verified with
# `git check-ignore -v`; `apps/leaf/example/leaf.example.json` does NOT end in `.leaf.json`
# and is deliberately still tracked.
*.leaf.json
*.tree.json
# A harvest document holds verbatim commit subjects clustered out of a REAL repository's
# history — same class of personal data as a leaf document, and covered the same way: BOTH the
# naming convention and the bare default name `harvest.mjs` writes are ignored everywhere, so
# the documented way of writing one is safe wherever it is written.
*.harvest.json
# A publish policy document. Less obviously personal than a leaf and ignored for the same reason:
# a rule compiled by example carries the fact it was compiled from — a commit sha, an evidence
# source, an id prefix — plus the subject's own sentence saying why they refused it. Committed,
# it would be a public list of exactly what somebody decided not to publish, which is worse than
# the work itself. Covered the same way as the rest: both the naming convention and the bare
# default name are ignored everywhere, so writing one anywhere is safe by default.
*.policy.json
# THE BARE DEFAULT NAMES, UNANCHORED — what the deleted anchors used to half-cover.
#
# The two-layer defence this block USED TO state — "the DEFAULT locations are anchored above and
# below, and these NAMING CONVENTIONS are ignored everywhere so the documented way of writing one
# is safe by default" — is quoted here in the past tense because the sentence is gone from the top
# of the block: it described the anchors this change deletes. The conventions ignored everywhere
# were only the DOTTED forms
# (`*.tree.json`, `*.leaf.json`, `*.harvest.json`, `*.policy.json`). The bare names ARE the
# documented way — they are literally what the tools write by default — and they were anchored to
# their default locations alone, so the same file one directory over was committable.
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-04 with `git check-ignore -v`: `apps/canopy/deploy/tree.json` NOT IGNORED,
# `some/where/leaf.json` NOT IGNORED, `tools/x/harvest.json` NOT IGNORED, `a/b/policy.json` NOT
# IGNORED — while `apps/canopy/deploy/foliage.tree.json` was caught by `*.tree.json`. Found while
# preparing a Vercel deployment, whose plan puts a real tree manifest at
# `apps/canopy/deploy/tree.json`: the one filename in the one directory that escaped the defence,
# made load-bearing by a spec. A roster is an organization's list of real people.
#
# Nothing in the tree carried any of these four names when this was written, so nothing was
# untracked by adding them. `gitignoreDefence.test.mjs` is now the gate, because this block's own
# claim to have been "verified with git check-ignore" had nothing holding it.
tree.json
leaf.json
harvest.json
policy.json

# A SOURCES document is the fifth garden document, and it is not less sensitive for being small:
# every entry carries an ABSOLUTE path into somebody's home directory and the email addresses they
# commit under. It exists so an unattended harvest knows where to read, which means it is written
# by a background agent on a machine nobody is watching — exactly the document most likely to be
# committed by accident. Covered the same two-layered way as its four siblings: the bare name that
# `apps/leaf/sources.mjs` actually writes, and the dotted convention anywhere else.
sources.json
*.sources.json
scarlet-model.json
# The precondition the block above states — nothing in the tree carried the name — does NOT hold
# for this one: `tools/vault/sources.json` was already tracked (the vault's declared roots, read by
# `tools/vault/build.mjs`, ~-relative by its own header so it names no home directory). The
# carve-out is exactly that file; `gitignoreDefence.test.mjs` fails on any tracked file a rule shadows.
!tools/vault/sources.json

# Canopy device credentials. Both the default and the documented connection names stay ignored
# wherever FOLIAGE_HOSTED_STATE puts them; inside a garden, `hosted/` below covers the rest.
connection.json
*.connection.json

# EVERY OTHER FILE A GARDEN HOLDS. A garden is a directory (`~/.foliage`, or wherever `--garden`
# or FOLIAGE_GARDEN puts it), and the tools write more into it than the documents above. Measured
# on fa29203 with a garden at `nested/garden/` inside a checkout: the documents were ignored and
# `workflow/workflow.json`, `published/*`, the media objects, the owner's email in
# `allowed_signers`, and full copies of the leaf and harvest left by `migrate-queue --apply` were
# all committable. `gitignoreDefence.test.mjs` lists each file with the code that writes it.
#
# The default garden, wherever a home directory or a copy of one lands.
.foliage/
# Directories a garden owns outright. Nothing tracked uses these names (the shadow test fails if
# a tracked file ever does). A NEW source directory with one of them is ignored too — `git add`
# says so — and needs a `!` carve-out like the one for `tools/vault/sources.json` above.
workflow/
published/
hosted/
# The media store also writes its own `*` .gitignore when it creates itself; this covers a store
# copied without it. Scoped to the store's fan-out because `media/` alone is a common source name.
**/media/sha256/
# Files a garden keeps beside its documents.
ladybug.json
private-brain-receipts.jsonl
allowed_signers
app.log
grow.log
# Copies of garden documents: migrate-queue backups, half-written temps, and locks.
*.before-adopt-*.json
*.local-lock
*.json.lock
*.json.lock.owner.*
*.tmp
*.tmp-*
# Not covered: `site/`, which `foliage export` writes by default. It holds only shared work, is
# meant to be hosted, and `site` names tracked source here.


# A harvest document is the airlock BEFORE review: machine-drafted prose about a real
# person's real work plus verbatim commit evidence. More sensitive than a leaf if
# anything, and covered the same two-layered way — the naming convention everywhere,
# plus the app's default output path anchored.
*.harvest.json
/apps/photosynthesis/leaf.harvest.json
/data/
/output/
/uploads/
*.sqlite
*.sqlite-wal
*.sqlite-shm

